#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570825} :activity/id #uuid "bf3715de-9981-56de-b88a-b45faa06a040" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050158 :topic/id #uuid "32af04f7-8bd5-5a90-8e7c-6c111c983ee8" :topic/math-academy-id 1955} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d6e4604-362d-57a9-a29b-e6f955c20002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499720} {:answer/id #uuid "7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499866} {:answer/id #uuid "821cfb95-3293-5dd2-ae19-a95141c69336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499901} {:answer/id #uuid "a05b6b84-89fc-51b8-b22e-5a5a84b42efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499964} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fb22f8-31af-560a-a251-6124b9e4d0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499866} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a71cca-98d2-5245-a4d1-fd31406052e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac300975-45c9-5a2c-a8b5-5d2addf48a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-132595" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3b1c7167-cdc6-57e2-a131-51fa45bc430d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5db68e00-0fe1-52aa-9d6b-1339f0b2d290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570288} {:answer/id #uuid "71ba467b-f837-5940-861f-031a2bb1bdfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570327} {:answer/id #uuid "968d7b90-c1b7-584f-85fa-c61f8ad5605f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570386} {:answer/id #uuid "97246dec-ee18-59de-9d5f-9526af928b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570389} {:answer/id #uuid "aae9a8b0-d1ae-5e09-af38-db030076ac47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570386} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f1aab5e-5cef-5890-b716-82e71a46cb8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4cb48e08-10b0-5815-97ab-c688f0460cd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132606" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{y}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{(1/2)} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,\\frac{1}{2}) & ={f}_{y}(2,\\frac{1}{2})(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4(y-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =4y-2 \\\\ z & =4y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2 \\\\ z &=4y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4y-1$ (or $4y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,4,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,4,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,4⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "3f73b412-2419-5b76-8ea4-608373bc04e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f49be2f-9fbd-57d9-8743-c7f792e14929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407305} {:answer/id #uuid "40205211-9c82-5bbc-b29c-5211d1a45bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-2y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407306} {:answer/id #uuid "645649e7-f6ca-5dcf-a129-028646be2cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407308} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3f6706-e60e-5d15-b360-58385706ee4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-2y+1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407309} {:answer/id #uuid "d4018ef0-2dba-5e3f-8527-3ea960bf4de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e94262ee-f4dd-5ac5-a015-9c5cf4798aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "05b78370-0b37-5800-aa28-6b6b17964b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-71216" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,0,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0)}(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1 \\\\ z &=-y-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5cb51352-36a7-5451-aa46-d3ee99b83611" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98ed7ce5-304c-5eac-b1cb-5f8c4b4d23e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,3,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383316} {:answer/id #uuid "4d34dabe-6639-51a1-abc1-40a551267c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383317} {:answer/id #uuid "7f96ce17-18b2-5a3f-8e28-936aabc05996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383318} {:answer/id #uuid "d7fe8dda-e437-53fb-9f60-09b8a339e900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,1,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383319} {:answer/id #uuid "650c5858-626f-56bb-bae0-7ffa49b7eab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383317} :answer-field/id #uuid "51a3324e-1ddd-5ca6-b734-63094c92f926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d71daf7a-6da8-5e30-8340-64d86d4d3ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-132609" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & ={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)(y-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(y-1) \\\\ z & =2y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2y-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-2$ (or $2y-z-2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "80f9b444-9cfd-5f24-8e33-f6319036fdd0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047539be-1b12-5b55-a1be-3e4cf9e127fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406792} {:answer/id #uuid "43cdbb4c-51d2-5f61-9d40-d04783c14422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406793} {:answer/id #uuid "60dae67c-4a44-58da-b15e-79beacb9c712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,0,\\sqrt{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406794} {:answer/id #uuid "652341d7-abb3-51cc-926c-835644238288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406795} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f93202-6d15-5d8b-9e8d-6d60c947ab0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406793} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b52ca0e-0824-536f-99e9-5701cb39c975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "320a3cd2-acfa-559d-b91b-9bfb6f0a3cde" :question/math-academy-id "q-71231" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =1(x-1) \\\\ z & =x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ z &=x-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-1$ (or $x-z+-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."} :step/id #uuid "f1a9160f-87bd-505a-a2a1-0157e9d7dd46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570809}}] :activity/title "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570112}]}
